  "query_preview": "..\nModels continue to be in good agreement of a strong pacific \nzonal jet stream stretching across the entire Pacific Ocean \nthrough the upcoming week bringing a series of weather systems \nacross the region. Models show three distinct weather systems to\npass through during the week. As each weather system passes the\njet stream over our region will take on a southwest-northeast\norientation resulting in a mild weather pattern with above\nnormal temperatures through the week. This flow pattern will\nalso tap into subtropical moisture at times leading to periods\nof moderate rain in the Cascades.\n\nSunday: The first system arrives this morning with swift mid \nlevel westerly flow as a cold front crosses the Cascades \nproducing a pronounced rain shadow across Central Washington. \nThen as the front encounters the higher terrain across Eastern \nWashington and North Idaho precipitation will redevelop with the\nhighest POP's over the ID Panhandle. Gusty winds will occur \nalong and behind the cold front with gusts of 20-30 MPH across \nthe region, and even stronger in the mountains with gusts of \n35-55 MPH. As snow levels drop to 4500 feet Sunday morning in \nthe Cascades rain will change to snow over Washington Pass with \n2 to 3 inches forecast. Other mountain passes across the region \nwill see little to no snow accumuation due to the high snow\nlevels.\n\nMonday through Tuesday: The next surge of precipitation arrives\nby Monday evening and this one the 850mb winds back to the south\nand southeast leading to less downslope flow off the Cascades\nwith widespread light precipitation developing. Snow levels\nMonday night climb to 5000 feet near the Canadian border and\n7000 feet over SE WA into the Central Panhandle Mountains. Then\nheading into Tuesday the lower level flow increases out of the\nsouthwest as another surge of precipitation enters, with the\nmoisture plume aimed at the Cascades, and skirting northern\nWashington into the Idaho Panhandle. Snow level continue to\nclimb reaching 7000-8000 feet regionwide as a warm front passes.\n\nWednesday and Thursday: A mild and dry period is in store\nfollowing the Tuesday warm front as the cold front stalls off\nthe coast. Areas of fog and stratus are expected but overall the\nmild air mass will mean well above normal temperatures \nespecially in the mountains.\n\nFriday: The third system of the week pushes in leading to\nanother round of precipitation especially for the Cascades. Snow\nlevels remain high, around 6000-7000 feet. \n\nAs far as impacts go in this pattern, we continue to monitor the\nburn scars in the Cascades. With the passage of each system the\nNBM is carrying a 30-50% chance of 24 hour rain amounts\nexceeding an inch near the Cascade crest. The good news is that\nthere will be breaks in the precipitation which should help keep\nriver levels in check. The one exception to monitor is the\nStehekin River which has a 22 percent chance of reaching flood \nstage by Thursday. High elevation snow melt and periods of rain\ncould result in localized debris flows or rockslides near burn \nscars. JW",
